<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="99cfeddf">&lt;h3&gt;
    Capacity based planning
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In capacity based planning, the Scrum team decides the number of hours each of the team members can dedicate to work.
    Consider the following variables in this process:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Length of working day (e.g. 8 hours per day)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Length of the sprint
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Any planned periods when team members are unavailable (i.e. vacation, training)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Any time planned for Scrum ceremonies (i.e. sprint planning, review, retrospective, Product Backlog refinement)
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Taking these variables into account, determine each team member&apos;s capacity for a sprint. Sum all of the Development
    Team members&apos; capacity estimates to arrive at their overall capacity for the sprint.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Once the team&apos;s capacity is estimated, discuss Backlog Items one after another with the Scrum team in the order
    proposed by the Product Owner. Have the Development Team draft a list of tasks for each Backlog Item and estimate them
    in hours. After this is done, the Development Team may decide to re-estimate any Backlog Item based on knowledge gained
    during discussions, if necessary. The sum of all task estimates across all discussed Backlog Items is then compared to
    the capacity of the Development Team. Once the capacity is filled, the relevant Backlog Items are included in the
    Sprint Backlog and all remaining candidate Backlog Items stay in the Product Backlog for consideration in the next
    sprint planning meeting.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Velocity based planning
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In velocity based planning, obtain information on past performance such as the Development Team&apos;s velocity (average
    number of Backlog Items completed in the past few sprints). Next, discuss Backlog Items in the order proposed by the
    Product Owner. Similar to capacity based planning, draft tasks and re-estimate Backlog Items as needed. Task estimation
    is not mandatory in this approach, nonetheless it is recommended in order to gain a better understanding of the work to
    be done and allow for coordination between Development Team members.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Once the sum of Backlog Item estimates across all discussed Backlog Items nears velocity, the relevant Backlog Items
    are included in the Sprint Backlog and all remaining candidate Backlog Items stay in the Product Backlog for
    consideration in the next sprint planning meeting.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Commitment based planning
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In commitment based planning, discuss Backlog Items with the Development Team in the order proposed by the Product
    Owner. Draft a list of tasks for each Backlog Item with the option to also estimate the tasks in hours. Once the tasks
    for any Backlog Item have been drafted, the Development Team may decide whether to re-estimate the Backlog Item. Next,
    have the Scrum Master ask the Development Team whether they can commit to completing each Backlog Item in addition to
    those previously discussed. Start with the first Backlog Item, to which the Development Team should have no difficulty
    committing seeing as it is the only piece of work thus far. Add the committed Backlog Item to the Sprint Backlog. For
    each subsequent Backlog Item discussed, the answer becomes less obvious as the Development Team&apos;s capacity slowly fills
    up. Finally, the Development Team will reach a point where they decide not to commit to completing a Backlog Item. With
    this, planning ends and non-committed Backlog Items remain in the Product Backlog.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
